WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:04.480
It takes a lot of work from a lot of people
to make a big Hollywood blockbuster.

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:09.120
While long days and arduous labor are par
for the course, a film set is usually a well-oiled

00:00:09.129 --> 00:00:09.640
machine.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:12.740
But humans are imperfect, and life is unpredictable.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.129
On rare occasions, tragedy strikes.

00:00:15.129 --> 00:00:19.180
Actors get injured, stuntmen pay the ultimate
sacrifice, and crew members end up in harm's

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:19.680
way.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:23.640
Here's a look at a few on-set incidents that
almost cost crew members their lives.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:24.940
Sliver

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:30.760
The success of Basic Instinct in 1992 left
filmgoers with a taste for more erotic thrillers

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:32.180
starring Sharon Stone.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:37.120
When Sliver hit theaters in 1993, it offered
a fairly straightforward mix of intrigue and

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:41.840
eroticism to tell a story about the rampant
voyeurism in a fancy New York apartment building.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:46.020
But the film was also supposed to include
some aerial footage of a Hawaiian volcano.

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:50.340
According to the LA Times, during the shoot,
a helicopter lost power and crashed on the

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:54.000
floor of a crater, a couple of hundred feet
from a pond of molten lava.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:58.820
A helicopter team rescued the pilot a few
hours later, while rainstorms and toxic volcano

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:01.410
fumes delayed the recovery of the cameramen.

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:05.359
The next day, one of them crawled out to safety,
while the other one had to spend two days

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:07.020
on a small ledge before his airlift.

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:08.520
Point Break

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:14.400
In 2015, the Hollywood remake machine spent
an astounding $105 million to give the world

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.460
another take on the 1991 action thriller Point
Break.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:24.100
Shortly after filming began in Tahiti in September
2014, one of the stunt surfers suffered a

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:27.289
totally bogus wipeout and severely hurt himself.

00:01:27.289 --> 00:01:31.520
The production hired Laurie Towner, one of
the most successful and famous surfers in

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:34.680
Australia, to film the surfing shots for actor
Luke Bracey.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:39.600
According to Australia's News.com, a giant,
punishing wave gave Turner what he described

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.659
as "a good old fashion flogging."

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:45.940
He ended up with a broken jaw, stitches in
his lip and eyelid, whiplash to his neck and

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:48.500
back, and wounds that just missed an artery.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:50.939
Resident Evil: The Final Chapter

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.920
2017's Resident Evil: The Final Chapter is
probably the last film in the Resident Evil

00:01:56.930 --> 00:02:01.090
series, and it is almost certainly the last
for veteran stunt performer Olivia Jackson.

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:05.939
On the first day of shooting, Jackson, standing
in for star Milla Jovovich, was all set to

00:02:05.939 --> 00:02:08.509
film a simple stunt involving a motorcycle.

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:12.680
She had to drive the bike in a straight line
while an oncoming camera on the arm of a mechanical

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:14.770
crane would lift up and sweep over her.

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:18.190
She told Glamour that she did her part, but
the camera didn't do what it was supposed

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:19.190
to do.

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:22.220
The arm failed to clear and struck Jackson
in the head and upper body.

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:25.220
Because the character wouldn't be wearing
a helmet, neither was Jackson.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:29.070
Skin was torn clean off her face, and she
suffered a severed neck artery.

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:33.350
She fell into a coma for two weeks, and she
was left with a huge scar across her face

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:36.380
and a paralyzed left arm, which was later
amputated.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:38.160
Charlie Wilson's War

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:45.100
2007's Charlie Wilson's War concerns the namesake
congressman's secret drive to fund the CIA's

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:49.360
hush-hush anti-Soviet Union tactics in Afghanistan
in the 1980s.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:53.480
Most of the movie consists of conversations,
but there is a little bit of action, including

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:57.290
a scene in which some stinger missiles were
supposed to shoot off a helicopter.

00:02:57.290 --> 00:03:01.530
While testing the device at Downey Studios
in Los Angeles, one of those stage missiles

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:06.390
unexpectedly exploded, injuring the special
effects assistant handling it, putting him

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:10.080
in critical condition for a time, according
to the Associated Press.

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:12.220
Blues Brothers 2000

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:17.500
1980's The Blues Brothers features one of
the most spectacular stunts in movie history.

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:21.120
Police chase Jake and Elwood and their "Bluesmobile"
through a Chicago mall.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:25.400
Filmed at the abandoned Dixie Square Shopping
Centre, the production wrecked the place,

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:27.180
along with 103 cars.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:31.420
Amazingly, no serious injuries resulting from
the controlled chaos were reported.

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:36.140
Blues Brothers 2000 tried to recapture the
magic of the original with some car chases

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:36.640
and crashes.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:41.100
Unfortunately, as Canadian site Jam! reported,
this time people got hurt.

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:45.750
While filming a smash-up, stunt performer
Bob Minor endured a serious head injury.

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:50.090
In an unrelated incident, the production enlisted
a towing company to remove an old car out

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:54.319
of a river, because they needed the waterway
clear to drive another car into it.

00:03:54.319 --> 00:03:58.970
The towing company got the car out with its
heavy machinery, only to drop it onto a crew

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:00.900
member's leg, which was later amputated.

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:01.960
Roar

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:06.750
Tippi Hedren is best known for starring in
Alfred Hitchcock's classic The Birds, but

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:10.600
later shifted away from a movie career and
into a life advocating for big cats.

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:14.300
She even founded an 80-acre animal sanctuary
in California.

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:19.220
Hedren apparently didn't harbor a grudge against
lions and other large kitties after the disastrous,

00:04:19.229 --> 00:04:22.840
somehow not-fatal shoot for the 1981 movie
Roar.

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:28.010
Ironically, the film is about a family terrorized
by wild animals, and it's legendary in Hollywood

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:29.800
for being extraordinarily dangerous.

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:33.040
That lore was salaciously exploited in the
marketing for Roar.

00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:36.490
The posters promised, "No animals were harmed
in the making of this film.

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:37.960
70 cast and crew members were."

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:46.960
It was a harrowing time, to say the least.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:50.430
However, Hedren stresses that the 70 number
is a bit overstated.

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:51.860
Speaking to Variety, she said,

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:54.880
"I think there were seven people that were
hurt, but not seriously."

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:58.939
Hedren herself got hurt, as did her daughter
and costar Melanie Griffith.

00:04:58.939 --> 00:05:03.620
Also, her husband at the time, director Noel
Marshall was in the hospital so many times

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:06.120
that she joked that "they were going to name
a wing after him."

